-- The Brand Bible structure, v2: the Head of Creative's review of the first Bibles and the reference Bible the agency
-- built by hand. Adds the cover, the unique selling proposition, the live pages ads send people to, the three lenses
-- on the audience, the voice of customer and the pain to angle matrix, the full positioning statement, a deeper review
-- bank, competitors with their links, ads and complaints, studies to use in ads, every Google size, links everywhere,
-- and keeps today's problems out of the Bible (they go to the team).
update public.ai_knowledge
set body = $body$One Brand Bible per client: the single source of truth every designer, editor, copywriter and media buyer builds from. It is a creative-development document about the brand: everything in it is true of the brand and evergreen unless it is dated ("What's changing", the calendar, offer history). Today's problems in the business or on the site (sold-out notices, broken pages, prices that disagree, a stock check) never go in the Bible: they go to "For the team" as "Current issue: …", and a lasting rule that follows from one ("don't show lobster tails until stock is confirmed") goes in as the rule, not the glitch.

Header: Client — Brand Bible · Version · Status (Draft / Reviewed by Kaizen / Sent to client / Client-approved / Updated since approval) · Developed date · Last updated · Reviewed by (Head of Creative, date) · Approved by (client contact, date). The cover (the overview field) carries who they are in one statement, their mission and vision in their own words (never written for them), and the goal they gave us.

Links: every page, profile, listing, ad, review, call, file and folder that has a URL is a Markdown link [name](url): the website and each landing page, socials, review pages, competitor sites, socials and Ad Library searches, the client's Drive folder and asset library, the creative database, call recordings, studies. A reader should be able to click straight through to do a deep dive.

Audience letters: profiles are lettered A, B, C… Wherever a letter appears outside the profile index (shoot list, review bank, matrix, calendar) write it with the name, e.g. "A · Fresh-Fish Seeker", so no one has to look it up. A profile with no review, angle or shot planned for it is a gap: say so.

**Read this first** (one page, plain text, one labeled block per line)
- What they sell: one sentence a stranger understands.
- Who we make creative for: the profiles, one line each ("A. Name: one line. Show: product.").
- The 10 rules that get creative rejected (most frequent first): a label line ending in a colon, then exactly ten lines numbered 1 to 10, each a rule with its source. Banned claims and words, required badges, products not to show, competitor and talent rules, look rules.
- Voice in one line plus one example line; look in one line (colors with hex, fonts, photo style).
- Platforms and sizes: every placement for every platform Kaizen runs. Meta: Feed 4:5 (1080x1350), Stories and Reels 9:16 (1080x1920). Google, when Kaizen runs Google: responsive display landscape 1.91:1 (1200x628), square 1:1 (1200x1200), portrait 4:5 (960x1200), logos 1:1 (1200x1200) and 4:1 (1200x300); Performance Max and Demand Gen the same images plus video in 16:9, 1:1 and 9:16; YouTube 16:9 (1920x1080) and Shorts 9:16; headlines 30 characters, long headline 90, descriptions 90. TikTok 9:16. Never "per the media buyer".
- The 3 best reviews for ads: picked from the review bank (4b), named by RV#, word for word. They are the strongest three, not the first three.

**1. What the client wants from advertising**
Services Kaizen provides and platforms; goals and KPI targets in the client's words (revenue goal, break-even, cost per result, return) with the call or form they came from; what success looks like; what they worry about. Key calls: date · type · what was decided, one line each, linked to the recording.

**2. The business**
What they sell; origin story in their words and whether they want it in ads; mission and vision in their words; **Unique selling proposition**: how they actually do it differently (sourcing, process, delivery, team), in plain words a copywriter can turn into a hook, e.g. "whole fish from around the world, broken down, sealed and shipped from their own facility: the fishmonger to your door"; how they sell (channels, consultation or checkout, deposits, decision time, follow-up the ads hand off to); where ads send people: page (link) · what it promises · price shown · action; offer history (newest first: date · offer · channel · notes, incl. offers the client won't run); product or service table: line · what it is · price as listed · key specs · status (push / show / don't show); what's changing (business changes, dated); next 12 months (dated, refreshed every month).

**3. Audience profiles (3–5)**
- 3 minimum, 5 maximum; fewer and sharper beats more and vaguer. Evidence from at least two of: intake, calls, client comms, reviews, ad or sales data, with a source on every line. Weight: customer data > reviews > calls and intake > client comms > competitor targeting > inference. Confidence: Proven (sales or ad data) · Likely (strong evidence, untested) · Hypothesis (to test). Letters are permanent; retired profiles stay listed with date and why.
- The key finding first: where who buys today, who responds to ads and who the client wants disagree (a three-lens table: lens · what it shows · finding).
- Index: Letter · Name · One line · Product to show · Confidence.
- Voice of customer: buyers' own words from forums, Reddit, review sites and competitors' reviews: quote · type (pain, desire, objection, regret) · profile · source link. Never used as the client's reviews.
- Pain to angle matrix: # · pain (their words) · profile · angle · promise · proof (RV#, facts, assets) · format that fits. 6–10 rows. Every concept in a creative brief traces back to a row.
- Per profile: Who they are · A day in their life · What they want (their words) · What hurts (their words) · What stops them buying (objection + our answer) · Awareness level · Triggers · Where they are · Angles that fit (3–5, example hook each) · Visual cues · Never show them · Evidence · Confidence.

**4. Brand voice and messaging**
Positioning in one sentence; the full positioning statement ("For [who] who [need], [brand] is the [category] that [promise], unlike [alternatives] that [weakness], because [reasons]"); reasons to believe: reason · proof with source (this is where the highest claims live, e.g. "the highest-quality seafood you can buy", when the client says it and can back it); 3–5 personality traits as "this, not that"; voice rules; words and lines the brand uses (and the words it uses instead of others: "collection" not "bundle"); lines the client approved and rejected, in their words, with why; claims in three columns: Approved (exact wording) · Needs client sign-off · Banned; required on every asset; Do / Don't table (10+ rows).

**4b. Review bank**
15–25 real reviews, 4–5 stars, that say something specific (a result, a feeling, a comparison, a moment), 15–40 words preferred, and that use the words buyers search with. Columns: RV# · review word for word · stars · shown as (first name + last initial · source) · source, date, link · profile (letter + name) · angle · Use in ads (OK / Needs client sign-off / Can't use, with why). Shorten only by cutting with "…" without changing meaning. Check each against the claims table. Note which the client features on their site. No full names or reviewer photos without permission. It grows over time: new strong reviews are added on every update.

**5. Look and feel**
Brand version in force (linked); colors (hex), fonts, logo rules; photo and video style; music; UGC and creator rules (and any in-house creator the client has); creative the client loved and creative they rejected: date · piece · their words · what it teaches, from the creative database, review comments, Slack, email and calls. When someone on the client side gets excited about a piece, it's here. Asset library: linked folders.

**5b. Assets, production and AI permissions**
What Kaizen makes; what the client provides; AI permissions table (AI images · AI edits to real photos · AI video · AI voiceover · AI people/avatars: Approved / Not approved / Not asked, limits, who said it when, source); asset inventory (type · what exists · newest · usable for), with links; gaps; shoot list: # · what to shoot · why (the profile, letter + name, and angle it unlocks) · who shoots it · specs and shots · priority.

**6. Competitors and their creative**
Table: brand (linked) · positioning · price · key claims · look and tone · how our client differs · links (landing page, Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, Ad Library). Creative they run now, longest-running first: brand · the promise · angle · our profile it targets · running since · Ad Library link. What their customers complain about: competitor · complaint word for word · source link · the angle it opens for our client. Category conventions (what everyone does) and the white space our client can own. Never "to research": research it, or say what couldn't be read.

**7. Market and rules of the road**
Category size and trends; research to use in ads: stat · source (linked study, survey or report) · how creative could use it, tagged [certain] / [likely] / [guessing]; regulation and ad-platform policy that limit what creative can say or show; seasonality.

**8. Marketing moments calendar (12 months)**
Date · moment · fit (High / Medium / Skip) · angle idea · asset-ready-by (moment minus 14 days) · notes (incl. moments the client bans or skips and why).

**9. Working together**
Client contacts (role, what they approve) and the Kaizen team by role (Head of Creative, graphic designer, video editor, copywriter, customer success, media buyer); approval flow (the Head of Creative reviews, then the client approves; never auto-approved); where feedback lives (linked); dated creative feedback log in the client's own words, newest first.

**10. Creative learnings** (grows over time)
Plain-language lessons that held for 14+ days of live spend. No metrics. Date · learning · applies to · evidence dates.

**11. Open questions**: two tables, For the client and For Kaizen: # · neutral question · why it matters · who answers · raised · status. Then the tests the creative will answer (not questions).
**12. Sources**: everything used, with dates read, linked; and what couldn't be read.
**Change log**: date · version · what changed · trigger · needs client confirmation (Y/N).

Quality bar: Read this first fits one page with the ten rules as their own numbered block; every section sourced, dated and linked; 3–5 profiles with 2+ sources and a confidence; a pain to angle matrix; claims split three ways; a review bank of 15+ that's claim-checked; competitors researched with links and complaints; no "to research" left; nothing point-in-time outside dated sections; no ad metrics anywhere.$body$
where agent_key = 'brandbible' and kind = 'skill' and title = 'Brand Bible structure, section by section';

-- Calls are the backbone: the client's own words on recorded calls hold what no website says
update public.ai_agents
set instructions = replace(
  instructions,
  'Build it for a brand-new client from sources every client has: the marketing intake form, the sales call, the website, Google and website reviews, and web research. Anything Kaizen already holds (Slack, email, Drive, client memory, past creative and feedback) sharpens it but is never the backbone.',
  'Build it from everything there is: the marketing intake form, every recorded call with the client (sales, onboarding, strategy and creative calls, transcripts included), the website, reviews, web research on the market, and everything Kaizen holds (Slack, email, Drive, client memory, the client''s verdicts and comments on our creative). The client''s own words on calls are the backbone: they hold what no website says (what makes the product the best, how it''s really made, who on their side creates content). A brand-new client with no calls yet is built from the intake, the website, reviews and research.'
)
where key = 'brandbible';
